 I was like three days after I  turn 18 and I was like going to New York  and gonna be a big star and it just  didn't occur to me that that wouldn't  happen and so it was either my naiveté  or my my fearlessness I guess that made  everything happen that has happened for  me and a lot of luck probably have you  been at all struck by the historical  differences between the character that  you play in the 60s and the current  roles that women get the play I think  that a lot of great things have happened  for a woman um since then I think that  we still have a long way to go I think  we put a lot of pressure on ourselves um  should have and do it all well and uh  but I think that for Betty she's doing  exactly what she wanted to do and she's  doing exactly what she was told she  should do she where the consequences I  think so I think that she is very um  educated and I think she's very  knowledgeable I mean Betty's only  character we ever see read anything that  has anything to do with the feminist  movement she reads The Feminine Mystique  by Betty Friedan she's only one that  does that and and yet we feel like she's  the most unempowered woman on the show  but she she's there's one episode where  she wants to rekindle her modeling  career and Don kind of puts the kibosh  on that and but there's there's no  reason why she couldn't have done it  when she married Henry Francis so I feel  like she's where she wants to be but for  there was a lot less opportunity than  especially educationally for women is  there a particular actor actress  director or someone that you wish was  still alive that you could have worked  with this or a favorite that you have  that you keep in the back of your mind I  would think maybe Marilyn Monroe she had  great comic timing I love she seems  funny
 

